WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.410 --> 00:00:06.620
The Plaintiff: Tom the Landlord, who is seeking
to evict his tenant over $850 of unpaid rent.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:11.660
The Defendant: Vera the Tenant, who admits
falling behind on rent, but says she shouldn’t

00:00:11.660 --> 00:00:12.940
lose her home for it.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:18.605
We’ll hear their stories and see what the
judge has to say on Eviction Court: Two Cents edition!

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.070
A landlord can’t just kick a tenant out
on a whim…

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:29.769
They need to get the OK from the state, and
the way they do that is through a civil process

00:00:29.769 --> 00:00:33.680
known as eviction, or “summary ejection.”

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:38.469
A landmark study by Princeton sociologist
Matthew Desmond estimated that there are almost

00:00:38.469 --> 00:00:40.860
a million evictions a year in America.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:44.930
That’s 1 out of every 17 renter households.

00:00:44.930 --> 00:00:49.450
There are many different reasons a landlord
can seek eviction, from lease violations to

00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:52.559
criminal activity to just wanting to sell
the house.

00:00:52.559 --> 00:00:55.814
But the vast majority are over unpaid rent.

00:01:01.822 --> 00:01:05.080
Vera's story is sadly familiar these days.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.760
She was working two jobs, in retail and food
service, and when the pandemic struck, both

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:11.830
businesses closed their doors.

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:15.970
She managed to stay afloat with the increased
unemployment benefits from the CARES Act…

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:18.250
until they expired in July.

00:01:18.250 --> 00:01:23.180
Her restaurant reopened shortly afterwards,
but with drastically shortened hours.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.720
With less than half the income she used to
have, she started to fall behind, and only

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.610
ten days after she failed to pay her rent
on time, she found an eviction notice taped

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:33.610
to her door.

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:37.830
There’s an assumption that you have to be
deep in debt to get evicted, but that’s

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.610
actually quite rare.

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:44.640
According to The Eviction Lab, the average
amount that triggers an eviction lawsuit is

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:47.060
less than one month’s rent.

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:51.500
If Vera can’t pay her debt within a few
days, she’ll be summoned to court at a specific

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:52.500
date and time.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:57.390
She’ll have one last chance to pay her debts
in full, plus late charges and legal fees,

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:01.960
or the court will order a sheriff to remove
her from the property.

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.450
Most defendants are highly motivated to pay
the money, if they can.

00:02:05.450 --> 00:02:09.860
They’re probably already living in the most
affordable unit they could find, and moving

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:14.590
into a new place means putting down a security
deposit they almost certainly don’t have.

00:02:14.590 --> 00:02:17.580
And that’s assuming they’d get approved.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:22.160
Most landlords won’t even consider a tenant
with an eviction on their record.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:29.030
This leverage creates a temptation for landlords
to use the eviction system as a kind of state-sponsored

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:32.530
debt collections agency, instead of a last
resort.

00:02:32.530 --> 00:02:36.000
Depending on the state, filing an eviction
lawsuit can be pretty cheap.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.670
There are even automated apps that streamline
the process.

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:44.020
And the added fees mean extra profit for the
landlord.

00:02:44.020 --> 00:02:49.260
This has led to a pattern of serial evictions,
where the same tenants are hauled into court

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:55.470
month after month, often paying more than
20% extra on their rent every time they fall

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:56.470
behind.

00:02:56.470 --> 00:03:00.950
So, there’s definitely potential for landlords
to abuse the system, but at least the government

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:04.569
stepped in this year to put a hold on evictions
during the COVID crisis.

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:10.110
Yes, the CDC announced a federal moratorium
on evictions until at least the end of the

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:13.460
year, because, y’know, it’s hard to quarantine
without a home.

00:03:13.460 --> 00:03:15.340
But there are some conditions.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:20.380
It only applies to tenants making less than
100 grand a year, and the tenant must declare

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:25.880
in writing that they experienced a loss of
income from COVID, that they have nowhere

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:30.069
else to go, and that they tried their best
to find financial assistance.

00:03:30.069 --> 00:03:34.540
The moratorium doesn’t mean that Vera’s
rent is forgiven--only that she can’t be

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:37.440
removed from the premises until 2021.

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:42.370
Her landlord is still free to bring her to
court before then, where he can contest her

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:47.910
claims, change his pretext to something not
covered by the moratorium, like lease violations,

00:03:47.910 --> 00:03:52.510
or just hope the tenant hasn’t heard about
the moratorium and will leave on her own.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:58.190
Tom the Landlord has opted to challenge Vera’s
declaration, which may sound cold-hearted,

00:03:58.190 --> 00:04:01.296
but let’s take a closer look at his side
of the story...

00:04:06.914 --> 00:04:11.950
10 years ago, after an injury left Tom unable
to keep working as a plumber, he decided to

00:04:11.950 --> 00:04:17.370
use his savings as a down payment on two rental
properties and become a full-time landlord.

00:04:17.370 --> 00:04:21.530
Unfortunately, the job turned out to be a
bit harder than he anticipated.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:26.169
Like many “mom-n-pop” real-estate investors,
the rent Tom collects from his tenants barely

00:04:26.169 --> 00:04:30.450
covers his mortgages, property taxes, upkeep
and personal living expenses.

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:35.080
But, he figures that the increase in home
values will at least allow him to sell the

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:38.389
properties in 20 years for enough to retire
on.

00:04:38.389 --> 00:04:40.520
And then along came COVID.

00:04:40.520 --> 00:04:46.150
Landlord associations across the country are
mounting legal challenges to the CDC’s moratorium,

00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:51.470
arguing that it essentially forces property
owners to subsidize their tenant’s housing.

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:56.130
The CARES Act provided some relief for owners,
allowing them to defer their mortgage payments

00:04:56.130 --> 00:05:01.860
for 6-12 months without incurring late fees,
but like the tenants, those debts aren’t

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:02.860
forgiven.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:07.310
When the forbearance period ends, Tom will
have to figure out how to pay everything he

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:09.760
owes or risk losing his properties.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:15.290
Tom’s done the math, and he just can’t
afford to rack up months of mortgage debt

00:05:15.290 --> 00:05:17.060
without any rental income.

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:21.750
As bad as he might feel for Vera, he doesn’t
think he should be the one to pay for her

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:22.750
problems.

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:27.360
So his lawyer makes the argument that Vera
hasn’t adequately searched for rental assistance

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:31.717
programs, and therefore doesn’t qualify
for the moratorium.

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:39.370
We’d like to say that Vera brought evidence
of her applications to numerous government

00:05:39.370 --> 00:05:44.480
housing grants that had already run dry, and
that the judge summarily dismissed the eviction

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:45.480
lawsuit.

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:46.480
Case closed!

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:47.480
But let’s get real.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:51.620
The majority of people who find themselves
in this situation don’t have the time or

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.080
information to mount a thorough defense.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:56.919
They can’t afford to bring lawyers with
them.

00:05:56.919 --> 00:06:01.940
Many aren’t even aware of their rights,
and most don’t show up to court at all,

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.430
meaning the landlord wins by default.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:09.330
And even though “mom and pop” landlords
like Tom are getting hit hard by the pandemic,

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:14.390
reports suggest that the majority of eviction
lawsuits are actually filed by large corporate

00:06:14.390 --> 00:06:19.831
landlords, owned by investment firms and hedge
funds that are better able to weather the storm.

00:06:19.831 --> 00:06:23.970
These firms have been somewhat restrained
by the moratorium, but they’re already preparing

00:06:23.970 --> 00:06:28.490
suits to evict thousands of tenants as soon
as the clock runs out.

00:06:28.490 --> 00:06:34.070
I suppose we could just blame the pandemic,
but unfortunately, the eviction crisis began

00:06:34.070 --> 00:06:36.360
well before COVID came along.

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:42.730
According to the Bureau of Labor Statistics,
hourly wages have increased only 5 to 25 percent

00:06:42.730 --> 00:06:48.460
over the last couple decades, while the cost
of renting has skyrocketed by 70 percent.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:53.840
While the number of rental units in America
has increased by 10.9 million, the number

00:06:53.840 --> 00:07:01.120
of units for $600 or less in inflation-adjusted
dollars has decreased by 4 million.

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:06.960
Essentially, all our new units are for high-income
renters, while low-income renters have vastly

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.030
fewer options to choose from.

00:07:09.030 --> 00:07:15.400
Today, the majority of renters who make less
than $30K a year are spending over half their

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:16.610
income on rent.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:21.360
So, if we’re looking for root causes of
America’s eviction epidemic, a lot of the

00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:24.750
blame goes to our lack of affordable housing.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:29.770
This is no easy problem to fix, with Congress
unable to agree on how to address it, and

00:07:29.770 --> 00:07:33.520
local communities opposed to any low-income
development in their neighborhoods.

00:07:33.520 --> 00:07:39.070
It’s a cold fact of capitalism that real-estate
developers can make more money building homes

00:07:39.070 --> 00:07:43.500
for rich people than poor people, so that’s
what they’ll continue to do.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:46.550
But the costs get shifted to the public.

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:51.919
States spend money processing eviction suits,
landlords have to find new tenants, cities

00:07:51.919 --> 00:07:57.290
have to expand social services and homeless
facilities, and of course, families are losing

00:07:57.290 --> 00:08:01.290
their homes, and with them, their ability
to find work.

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:05.240
All over debts that are usually less than
$1000.

00:08:05.240 --> 00:08:10.180
As with most systemic problems, looking for
blame is less productive than looking for

00:08:10.180 --> 00:08:11.190
solutions.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:15.110
As Clemson professor Elora Raymond put it,
“...is it the tenant’s fault… is it

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:16.210
the landlord’s fault?

00:08:16.210 --> 00:08:17.970
...I think it doesn’t really matter.

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:20.430
Because this doesn’t work.”

00:08:20.430 --> 00:08:21.949
And that’s our two cents.

00:08:23.296 --> 00:08:28.270
If you’re caught in a legal jam because
of unpaid rent or debts, there are organizations

00:08:28.270 --> 00:08:29.270
that can help.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:32.440
Check out our video guide to dealing with
debt collectors to learn more!

